package Lessons

use Libraries.Web.Page.all
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.ComputerScienceStandards2026
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.QuorumStandards

class TerrainGames2 is QuorumPage
    // The site's sets of standards, built once for every page.
    QuorumStandards standards

    on create
        SetLocation("experiment/Scenes/terrainGames.html")
    end

    action Setup
        // For search engines and link previews. An experiment, so it stays
        // out of search: the real lesson is the one to find.
        SetDescription("Learn how a game car works as a physics simulation, and drive a toy car on a Quorum Studio terrain.")
        SetSearchable(false)

        Title("Driving on Terrain: Vehicles and Physics")

        Overview()
        WarmUp()
        Explore()
        Engage()
        WrapUp()
    end

    action Overview
        Section("Overview")
        Paragraph("Racing games, delivery games and open-world adventures all have one thing in common: something with
wheels moving over the ground. In this tutorial, you will load a toy car into a scene with a terrain
and drive it over the hills you made. Along the way you will see how a game turns a car into
physics: a body with weight, four wheels on springs, an engine that pushes and brakes that hold, and
tires that grip until they slide. The car comes from AllTerrainVehicle, a Quorum test project for
driving over large terrains.")

        Subsection("Goals")
        Goals()
        ListItem("Explain how a game models a car: a body, wheels on springs, an engine, steering and brakes")
        ListItem("Load the toy car into a scene with a terrain, and drive it with the keyboard")
        ListItem("Describe how mass, springs, grip and the width between the wheels change the way a car drives")
        ListItem("Read a program built from small actions, and change one setting at a time to see what it does")
    end

    action WarmUp
        Section("Warm up")
        Paragraph("Think about riding in a car or on a bike over a bumpy road. What keeps you from feeling every single
bump? What makes a car skid on ice, or tip over on a sharp turn? Make a list of the parts of a real
car that matter for how it moves.")

        Subsection("Vocabulary")
        Paragraph("You will be learning about the following vocabulary words:")
        Table("Vocabulary", "media/scenes/Terrain/Vehicles/Vocabulary.csv")

        Subsection("New Code to Learn")
        Paragraph("You will be using the following new pieces of code:")
        Table("New Code to Learn", "media/scenes/Terrain/Vehicles/Code.csv")

        Subsection("CSTA Standards")
        ComputerScienceStandards2026 computerScience = standards:GetComputerScience2026()
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_PROGRAM_DEVELOPMENT_12)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_PROGRAM_DEVELOPMENT_13)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_DATA_INVESTIGATION_26)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:SPECIALTY_1_GAME_DEVELOPMENT_DESIGN_AND_DEVELOPMENT_3)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_PROGRAM_DEVELOPMENT_14)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:SPECIALTY_1_X_PLUS_COMPUTER_SCIENCE_X_PLUS_COMPUTER_SCIENCE_4)
        StandardsList(computerScience)
    end

    action Explore
        Section("Explore")
        Paragraph("A car in a game is a physics simulation, built from the same ideas as the falling ball in "):Link("Properties", "/experiment/Scenes/properties.html"):Text(": mass, forces, gravity and friction. What makes a car special is how those ideas are put together.
A heavy body sits on four springs, each spring rests on a wheel, the engine pushes the wheels, the
brakes hold them, and the tires grip the ground until they are pushed too hard. Quorum has classes
for each of these parts. In this tutorial you will load the toy car from AllTerrainVehicle, a Quorum
project for testing cars on large terrains, and look at how its program builds the car piece by
piece.")
        HowAGameCarWorks()
        GettingTheCar()
        TheProgram()
        Driving()
        HowTheProgramBuildsTheCar()
        WhyCarsTipAndSkid()
    end

    private action HowAGameCarWorks
        Subsection("How a game car works")
        Paragraph("A real car's wheels are round, and they roll. Simulating every wheel as a real round object,
touching the ground at just the right point and rolling without slipping, is hard for a physics
engine to do well, so many games use a shortcut called a "):Term("raycast vehicle"):Text(". Quorum's "):Code("Vehicle"):Text(" class is one.")
        Paragraph("In a raycast vehicle, the wheels aren't physics objects at all. Each wheel is an invisible line, a "):Term("raycast"):Text(", sent straight down from the car's body on every physics step. The line finds out how far away the
ground is under that corner of the car. A "):Term("spring"):Text(" then pushes the body up: the closer the ground, the more the spring is squeezed, and the harder it
pushes. This is the car's "):Term("suspension"):Text(", and it is why the body rides smoothly over bumps instead of bouncing off them. "):Term("Damping"):Text(" slows the spring down, the way a shock absorber does, so the car settles after a bump instead of
bouncing on and on.")
        Paragraph("The wheels also push the car along and turn it. The engine's force is applied where each driving
wheel touches the ground, the front wheels turn to steer, and each tire grips the ground sideways.
If a tire is pushed sideways harder than its grip, which Quorum calls its "):Term("friction slip"):Text(", it slides, and the car skids. The body you see is the car's model, moving with the physics body;
the wheels drawn on it are part of the model.")
    end

    private action GettingTheCar
        Subsection("Getting the car")
        Paragraph("The car is the "):Strong("Toy Car"):Text(", one of the free sample models the Khronos Group shares so that people can test 3D programs. The
Khronos Group is the same group that looks after OpenGL, WebGL and Vulkan, and they also created "):Strong("glTF"):Text(", the file format the car is saved in. The car model was made by Guido Odendahl, and its materials
and scene by Eric Chadwick, and they released it under the "):Strong("CC0"):Text(" license, so anyone may use it in any project.")
        Paragraph("The car's files are on GitHub, in the "):Link("Toy Car folder of glTF-Sample-Assets", "https://github.com/KhronosGroup/glTF-Sample-Assets/tree/main/Models/ToyCar"):Text(". That folder holds the model in more than one form; this tutorial uses the one in its "):Strong("glTF"):Text(" folder, which is a "):Code(".gltf"):Text(" text file, a "):Code(".bin"):Text(" file of shapes, and the images that color the car.")
        NumberedList()
        ListItem("In your project's folder, make a folder named "):Code("Car"):Text(" at the top of the project.")
        ListItem("Open the "):Link("glTF folder", "https://github.com/KhronosGroup/glTF-Sample-Assets/tree/main/Models/ToyCar/glTF"):Text(" in a web browser. It lists ten files:")
        NestedBulletList()
        ListItem():Code("ToyCar.gltf"):Text(" and "):Code("ToyCar.bin"):Text(";")
        ListItem():Code("ToyCar_basecolor.png"):Text(", "):Code("ToyCar_clearcoat.png"):Text(", "):Code("ToyCar_emissive.png"):Text(", "):Code("ToyCar_normal.png"):Text(" and "):Code("ToyCar_occlusion_roughness_metallic.png"):Text(";")
        ListItem():Code("Fabric_baseColor.png"):Text(", "):Code("Fabric_normal.png"):Text(" and "):Code("Fabric_occlusion.png"):Text(".")
        EndNestedList()
        ListItem("For each file, open it on GitHub and use its "):Strong("Download raw file"):Text(" button, then move the file into your "):Code("Car"):Text(" folder. All ten files must be in the same folder, because "):Code("ToyCar.gltf"):Text(" finds the others by name.")
        ListItem("Also download the folder's "):Code("LICENSE.md"):Text(", from the "):Link("Toy Car folder", "https://github.com/KhronosGroup/glTF-Sample-Assets/tree/main/Models/ToyCar"):Text(", into "):Code("Car"):Text(", so the license stays with the model.")

        Subsubsection("Centering the car")
        Paragraph("The toy car sits on a small blanket, and the model's center is not in the middle of the car's body.
For driving, the car needs a copy with the blanket left out and its center moved to the middle of
the body, because a physics box is centered on its item.")
        Paragraph("You don't make this copy by hand. The program on this page does it for you: the first time it runs,
its "):Code("MakeCenteredCar"):Text(" action reads "):Code("ToyCar.gltf"):Text(", changes it, and saves the result as "):Code("ToyCarCentered.gltf"):Text(" in the same folder. "):Quoted("How the program builds the car"):Text(" explains how.")
        Paragraph("Finally, use a scene with a terrain, such as one you shaped and painted in the earlier tutorials,
saved as "):Code("Scenes/Scene.qs"):Text(".")

        Subsubsection("Giving credit")
        Paragraph("The car is CC0, so credit isn't required, but it is good practice, as in "):Link("Terrain Materials and Painting", "/experiment/Scenes/terrainMaterials.html"):Text(". Add the car to your project's "):Code("Credits.txt"):Text(":")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/scenes/TerrainGames/Credits.txt", "")
    end

    private action TheProgram
        Subsection("The program")
        Paragraph("Replace the code in "):Code("Main.quorum"):Text(" with this program. It is QuincyWorld's program, a small version of a driving demo, with one action
added, "):Code("MakeCenteredCar"):Text(", to prepare the car:")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/scenes/TerrainGames/Main.quorum")
        Paragraph("Run it. The car drops onto the middle of the terrain, settles on its springs, and the camera follows
from behind and above.")
    end

    private action Driving
        Subsection("Driving")
        Table("Driving", "media/scenes/Terrain/Vehicles/DrivingKeys.csv")
        Paragraph("The "):Code("VehicleMovementBehavior"):Text(" turns these keys into three commands for the car: "):Term("throttle"):Text(", from full reverse to full forward; "):Term("steering"):Text(", from full left to full right; and "):Term("braking"):Text(". Every key can be changed. For example, to drive with the arrow keys, add these lines in "):Code("BuildVehicle"):Text(", before "):Code("AddBehavior"):Text(":")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/scenes/TerrainGames/ArrowKeys.quorum")
    end

    private action HowTheProgramBuildsTheCar
        // A double quote, for code that has one.
        text quote = ""
        quote = quote:GetDoubleQuote()

        Subsection("How the program builds the car")
        Paragraph("The program is made of small actions, each building one part, which makes it easier to read and to
change one thing at a time.")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Code("BuildChassis"):Text(" makes the body. It loads the toy car model and scales it up: the toy is about 4 centimeters long,
and a scale of 152 makes it about car-sized, with wheels about 0.4 meters in radius. It gives the
body a "):Strong("box"):Text(" for physics, a little smaller than the model so it doesn't catch on hills, and a mass of "):Strong("800 kilograms"):Text(". Its angular damping of 0.9 makes a tilt die down before it grows into a roll.")
        ListItem():Code("MakeCenteredCar"):Text(" prepares the model, once. A "):Code(".gltf"):Text(" file is plain text, written in JSON, like the scene and material files in "):Link("Creating a Scene", "/experiment/Scenes/create.html"):Text(". It lists the model's pieces, and each piece names its shape with a line such as "):Code(quote + "mesh" + quote + ": 0,"):Text(". The action reads the file as text and uses "):Code("Replace"):Text(" to change three of those lines. It adds a "):Term("translation"):Text(", a move along x, y and z, to the body (piece 0) and the glass (piece 2), lowering them a little
less than a centimeter in the model's own tiny units. It also takes the shape away from the blanket
(piece 1), so the blanket draws nothing. Then it writes the result to "):Code("ToyCarCentered.gltf"):Text(", which still uses the same "):Code("ToyCar.bin"):Text(" and images. If that file is already there, the action does nothing, so it only works once. To make
it again, delete "):Code("ToyCarCentered.gltf"):Text(".")
        ListItem():Code("BuildVehicle"):Text(" joins the body to four wheels. Each wheel is attached 1.6 meters to the side of the body's center,
2.06 meters ahead for the front wheels and 1.58 meters behind for the back ones. Only the front
wheels steer. It then adds the vehicle to the 3D layer, so the layer updates it on every physics
step, and gives the body the "):Code("VehicleMovementBehavior"):Text(", with an engine force of 5,000 newtons forward and 3,000 in reverse.")
        ListItem():Code("AddWheel"):Text(" makes one wheel. The ray points straight down, the spring is 0.4 meters long at rest, and the
wheel's radius is 0.4 meters. Its "):Strong("stiffness"):Text(" of 20 decides how far the springs sag under the car's weight; its "):Strong("damping"):Text(" settles the car after a bump; and its "):Strong("friction slip"):Text(" of 6 is its grip.")
        ListItem():Code("ResetVehicle"):Text(" puts the car upright in the middle of the terrain, a little above the ground, and stops it. "):Code("Update"):Text(" calls it when you press R, or when the car falls off the edge of the world.")
        ListItem():Code("FindTerrain"):Text(" and "):Code("GetGroundHeight"):Text(" find the scene's terrain and ask how high the ground is at a place, so the car can start just above
it.")
    end

    private action WhyCarsTipAndSkid
        Subsection("Why cars tip and skid")
        Paragraph("When a car turns, its tires pull it sideways, toward the inside of the turn. That pull happens at
the ground, but the car's weight is centered higher up, at its "):Term("center of mass"):Text(". The higher the center of mass, and the faster the turn, the more that sideways pull tries to roll
the car over its outside wheels. What holds it down is its weight, resting on those outside wheels.
The farther apart the wheels are, the harder the weight resists tipping.")
        Paragraph("The program's settings near the top are tuned for this:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Code("wheelSide"):Text(", 1.6, sets the wheels well out to each side. The test car in AllTerrainVehicle had them at 1.12,
and the wider track resists tipping about 40 percent harder.")
        ListItem():Code("rollInfluence"):Text(", 0, applies the tires' sideways pull at the height of the body's center instead of at the ground,
so turning doesn't roll the car at all. Real cars aren't that lucky; this is one of the places a
game makes driving easier than real life.")
        ListItem():Code("frictionSlip"):Text(", 6, lets the tires slide a little sooner, so a turn that is too hard makes the car skid rather than
flip.")
        ListItem():Code("maximumSteering"):Text(", 0.35, keeps the front wheels from turning too sharply.")
    end

    action Engage
        Section("Engage")
        Paragraph("In this activity, you and a partner load the toy car into a scene with a terrain and drive it
around. The goal is to get a working vehicle running in your own scene and feel how the physics
settings change the way it drives. Use a scene with a terrain that has some hills and valleys, such
as one you made in "):Link("Shaping Terrain", "/experiment/Scenes/terrainShape.html"):Text(" or "):Link("Generating Terrain", "/experiment/Scenes/terrainGenerate.html"):Text(". Follow "):Quoted("Getting the car"):Text(" to download the car from GitHub into a "):Code("Car"):Text(" folder and credit it, replace "):Code("Main.quorum"):Text(" with the program on this page, and run it. Take turns driving: one partner drives while the other
gives directions, such as "):Quoted("drive to the top of the nearest hill"):Text(", "):Quoted("go down into the valley and back up"):Text(", or "):Quoted("make a full circle without tipping over"):Text(". Press R whenever the car needs a fresh start.")
        Paragraph("When you've both had a turn, try changing one setting at a time, running the game again after each
change and writing down what you notice. For example:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("halve the engine force, from 5000 to 2500: can the car still climb the same hill?")
        ListItem("set "):Code("wheelSide"):Text(" back to 1.12, or "):Code("rollInfluence"):Text(" to 0.5: does the car tip more easily on sharp turns?")
        ListItem("set "):Code("frictionSlip"):Text(" to 2, then to 20: what happens to turning and skidding?")
        ListItem("double the suspension stiffness, from 20 to 40: does the ride over bumps feel different?")
        Paragraph("Put each setting back before trying the next, so you know which change did what.")
    end

    action WrapUp
        Section("Wrap up")
        Paragraph("A game car is a physics simulation built from parts: a heavy body, springs on invisible rays, an
engine, steering and tires with limited grip. Many of its numbers were chosen to make driving fun
rather than perfectly real, such as a roll influence of 0. Which of the settings you tried made the
car feel most realistic, and which made it most fun to drive? When would a game choose fun over
realism?")
    end
end
